{
  "name": "Overriding MIPS zero volumes in POPS by hand",
  "today": "For outside-measured and mirrored (bidirectional) meters, measurement's system (MIPS) gets no volume until month-end, so its nightly file sends POPS a zero. That zero becomes the best available volume (BAV) and replaces the estimate Gas Control sends. POPS then passes the zero to Sitara, so purchase expense drops out of the desk's P&L and sits in the UA4 account. Pipeline Operations schedulers have to spot these zeros, get Gas Control's volumes and override POPS, then re-allocate the meter. A later nightly roll can write the zero back over a corrected meter, as happened at Valero Texoma on 3/21/2000. When a zero is missed, it takes an escalation by email: on 3/21/2000 Lauri Allen, Charlotte Hawkins, Michael Eiben and Gary Anderson wrote about it, and on 4/4/2000 Robert Lloyd had to ask again for Enron Methanol because his first note was not read as a request. The POPS team reported the bug to MIPS, but was told it was not a priority.",
  "change": "Have the MIPS nightly file mark 'no data yet' as incomplete instead of zero, so POPS keeps Gas Control's estimate and schedulers stop overriding zeros by hand.",
  "explanation": "Measurement would have its MIPS vendor (SPI) change the nightly file so that a zero sent because no data has arrived is tagged incomplete. Gary Anderson in measurement named this as the only possible fix; SPI was looking into it, and the cost was not yet known and might need a work order. POPS would then keep Gas Control's estimate as the best available volume until a real measured volume arrives. Schedulers would no longer search for zeroed meters, retype estimates, re-allocate, or redo the work after a nightly roll. The P&L and UA4 would no longer be wrong during the month, and the escalation emails would stop.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/928.eml",
      "quote": "In the mean time we are overriding the volumes in POPS with the estimated volumes."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/928.eml",
      "quote": "This was corrected this morning and the meter re-allocated."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1013.eml",
      "quote": "Please override POP'S  daily volume{ presently zero}  to reflect  daily activity you can obtain from Gas Control."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1014.eml",
      "quote": "I have updated the info at Enron Methanol per gas control's volumes (via meter overrides)."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/936.eml",
      "quote": "This is an outside measured volume that we don't receive volumes on until month's end so systematically, no information equals zero.",
      "note": "The zeros arrive every night for these meters until month-end, so the overrides are recurring work, not a one-time fix."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/936.eml",
      "quote": "The only possible solution is to tag all zero volumes sent in the nightly file process as (i) incomplete.",
      "note": "Measurement's own proposed fix, which is the change proposed here. Its cost was still being investigated."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/936.eml",
      "quote": "When MIPS sends Pipeline Operations a zero prelim, this becomes the best available number and overlays the estimate that Gas Control sends us.  When this happens & scheduling doesn't catch to override the zero, the desk's expense is understated.",
      "note": "Why scheduling must check for and override the zeros, and what goes wrong when they miss one."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/936.eml",
      "quote": "We continue to have this problem at various bidirectional meters",
      "note": "Several meters are affected, not just one."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/928.eml",
      "quote": "This particular meter was correct last night, the 14th rolled last night from MIPS and overrode the BAV with a -0- from day 14 forward.",
      "note": "A nightly roll undoes earlier corrections, so the same meter has to be fixed again."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/925.eml",
      "quote": "This means that these purchase volumes (expenses) have not been picked up in Tom's P&L and are, instead, sitting in UA4.",
      "note": "The cost of a missed zero: the desk P&L is wrong until someone finds it."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/928.eml",
      "quote": "Our POPS team (Romeo D'Souza) has identified this problem and notified MIPS.  However, he was told this was not a priority item.",
      "note": "Where it stalls: the fix was known but not scheduled, so the hand work continued."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/farmer-d/logistics/1014.eml",
      "quote": "I did not realize that's what you wanted when you handed me the \"sticky\" on Monday.....  I thought you were just being informative.",
      "note": "Override requests passed by sticky note get missed, so a zero stayed in POPS for an extra day."
    }
  ],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": null,
  "minutes": 10
}

{
  "name": "Monthly Investor Relations volume-variance explanations",
  "change": "Pre-fill each region's previous explanation next to every +/-10% variance in the Statistical Database, so controllers tick 'same driver' or type a new one there instead of replying by email.",
  "reason": "The same work was already rejected in decisions.md for the same gaps, and this version repeats them: 11 controllers' time is costed from one controller's (Baumbach's) one- and three-line replies, 'answers repeat' rests on one region in two months, and the change drops the stated rule that 'the volumes be verified by commercial personnel' for repeated drivers.",
